<BlogPost slug="iptv-buffering-troubleshooting">
  <p>If your IPTV is not working, the cause is very often local. Prime IPTV is built for smooth, buffer-free viewing on premium servers. When a stream does stutter, freeze or refuse to load, the cause is very often local: the Wi-Fi, the device, the player settings or a small typo in the login. Work through this checklist from the top, because most problems are solved in the first few steps.</p>

  <h2>Part 1: Buffering and freezing</h2>

  <h3>1. Test whether it is one channel or everything</h3>
  <p>Open a different channel, then a different category. If only one channel misbehaves, note its name and the time and tell support. If everything buffers, the problem is probably on your side of the connection.</p>

  <h3>2. Check your internet speed</h3>
  <p>Run a speed test on the same device and network. As a rough guide, one stream needs roughly the following (see the full <a href="/iptv-internet-speed-requirements/">internet speed requirements</a> for several screens at once):</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>Approximate speed per stream</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>SD</td><td>5 Mbps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HD</td><td>10 Mbps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Full HD</td><td>15 Mbps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4K</td><td>25 Mbps or more</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>These are rough guide values, not guarantees. Multiply them by the number of screens streaming at once, and remember that other people in the house share the connection.</p>

  <h3>3. Use a cable if you can</h3>
  <p>Wi-Fi is the most common cause of buffering, especially in another room or on a crowded network. Connect your TV box or Firestick with Ethernet (a Fire TV Ethernet adapter works), or move closer to the router and use the 5 GHz band.</p>

  <h3>4. Restart the chain</h3>
  <p>Restart the player app, then the device, then the router, in that order. Unplug the router for 30 seconds before switching it back on.</p>

  <h3>5. Free up bandwidth</h3>
  <p>Pause large downloads and cloud backups, and check whether another screen is streaming 4K. Fewer heavy streams at the same time leaves more room for yours.</p>

  <h3>6. Adjust player settings</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Try a lower quality version of the channel (FHD instead of 4K, or HD instead of FHD).</li>
    <li>Increase the player's buffer size if it has that option.</li>
    <li>Switch hardware decoding on or off. One setting is often smoother on a given device.</li>
    <li>Update the player app, and clear its cache from the device's app settings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>7. Test without a VPN or proxy</h3>
  <p>If you normally run a VPN or proxy on the device or router, switch it off briefly to see whether it is slowing the stream.</p>

  <h2>Part 2: Login and "channels will not load"</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Re-enter the details carefully.</strong> Copy and paste the server URL, username and password from your WhatsApp or email message. Look for a trailing space, a missing "http://" or letters that look alike (0/O, l/I).</li>
    <li><strong>Use the right login method.</strong> If your player offers both Xtream Codes and M3U, use the one our message gave you. Smart TV and MAG apps may need MAC-address activation instead. See the <a href="/setup/smart-tv/">Smart TV</a> and <a href="/setup/mag/">MAG</a> guides.</li>
    <li><strong>Check your plan's connections.</strong> If more screens are streaming than your plan allows, extra streams may be refused. See <a href="/blog/how-many-iptv-connections-do-i-need/">how many connections you need</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the trial or plan period.</strong> A 24-hour trial ends after 24 hours; a paid plan ends at its end date.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm the device clock and internet.</strong> A wrong date or no internet can prevent a login from working.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Part 3: TV guide (EPG) problems</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Refresh or reload the EPG from your player's settings, and allow a few minutes to finish.</li>
    <li>Make sure the player's EPG option is switched on for your playlist.</li>
    <li>Catch-up (replaying earlier programmes) depends on the player you use, so check that your player supports it.</li>
  </ul>

